[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 1"""
 date="2026-11-06T18:11:13Z"
 content="""
Another approach would be to keep a bounded pool of PerRepoServerStates.
When a PerRepoServerState needs to be added to the pool and the
pool is full, first find one for a different repository that is not in use,
and remove it from the pool, stopping its helper processes.

This seems easier to implement than coordinating a process pool 
between Annex objects.

And it could also avoid problems with too many open files, since it
could also close open file handles in the Annex object such as the
keys db handle. I do think that running out of file descriptors would be
the next problem after too many processes when scaling p2phttp up to
serving a large number of repositories, so this might as well solve that
too.

There would still be worker pools for processes like `git hash-object`
that are shared amoung all threads serving the same repository,
scaling up to one per cpu. So long as the pool is capped at some reasonable
number (say 100), that is ok, it means something like `100 * (cpus + 1)`
processes, probably well under the maximum number of processes. Make
the maximum concurrent repositories served a tunable value.
"""]]
